# Frontend State Management Rules

## Related Rules
- @frontend-rules.mdc for general frontend guidelines
- @frontend/core/performance.mdc for performance considerations
- @frontend/core/testing.mdc for testing requirements

## Overview
This document defines the rules and best practices for managing state in Ruby on Rails frontend applications, primarily using Stimulus.js and Rails UJS patterns.

## State Management Principles

### 1. State Location
- Use Stimulus.js controllers for component-specific state
- Use Rails UJS for server-side state management
- Use data attributes for DOM state
- Use Rails session/cookies for persistent state
- Use Rails caching for performance optimization

### 2. State Organization
- Group related state in Stimulus controllers
- Use proper data attributes naming
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Use Rails partials for dynamic content
- Follow RESTful patterns for state updates

### 3. State Updates
- Use Stimulus actions for client-side updates
- Use Rails UJS for server-side updates
- Implement proper error handling
- Include loading states
- Handle edge cases explicitly

## Implementation Guidelines

### Stimulus.js Controllers
```ruby
# Good
class ToggleController < Stimulus::Controller
  static targets = ["content"]
  
  toggle() {
    this.contentTarget.classList.toggle("hidden")
  }
end

# Bad
class EverythingController < Stimulus::Controller
  # Too many responsibilities
  # Mixed concerns
end
```

### Rails UJS Implementation
```ruby
# Good
<%= link_to "Update", update_path, 
    class: "btn",
    data: { 
      remote: true,
      method: :patch,
      confirm: "Are you sure?"
    } %>

# Bad
<%= link_to "Update", update_path, 
    class: "btn",
    onclick: "updateEverything()" %>
```

## Performance Considerations
- Use proper caching strategies
- Implement proper asset precompilation
- Use Russian Doll caching
- Implement proper database indexing
- Use background jobs for heavy operations

## Testing Requirements
- Test Stimulus controllers
- Test Rails UJS interactions
- Test error states
- Test edge cases
- Test state persistence

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Not using proper caching
- Mixing concerns in Stimulus controllers
- Not using Rails UJS patterns
- Not handling loading states
- Not implementing proper error handling

## Validation Checklist
- [ ] State is properly organized
- [ ] Stimulus controllers are focused
- [ ] Rails UJS patterns are followed
- [ ] Error handling is implemented
- [ ] Loading states are handled
- [ ] Performance optimizations are in place
- [ ] Tests cover all state scenarios
- [ ] Edge cases are handled
